WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.700
Moodle security is layered risk reduction.

2
00:00:03.800 --> 00:00:11.000
No single setting makes a site universally secure, and no managed hosting arrangement removes the need for administrative choices.

3
00:00:11.100 --> 00:00:31.500
Administrators should maintain supported versions, monitor security announcements, keep plugins under review, limit administrator access, protect authentication flows, configure sessions deliberately, use secure transport, watch scheduled tasks, review logs, and maintain an incident process that names who decides and who communicates.

4
00:00:31.600 --> 00:00:36.400
The goal is to reduce predictable risk while keeping legitimate learning work possible.

5
00:00:36.680 --> 00:00:38.360
Least privilege remains central.

6
00:00:38.460 --> 00:00:52.300
Security problems often begin as ordinary convenience: too many site administrators, broad manager assignments, old plugin permissions, shared support accounts, unrestricted course creation, or unreviewed integrations.

7
00:00:52.400 --> 00:00:57.360
The administrator's job is not to block learning work, but to make the safe path usable.

8
00:00:57.460 --> 00:01:03.540
Access should match the role, context, and time period required for the task.

9
00:01:03.820 --> 00:01:07.660
Privacy administration is related to security, but not identical.

10
00:01:07.760 --> 00:01:18.880
Moodle includes tools and configuration areas that can support privacy requests, data export, retention, and policy communication, depending on version and configuration.

11
00:01:18.980 --> 00:01:22.020
Those tools are not automatic legal compliance.

12
00:01:22.120 --> 00:01:32.680
The organization still needs policy ownership, lawful basis decisions where applicable, retention schedules, request handling, escalation paths, and records of what was done.

13
00:01:32.960 --> 00:01:37.680
Logs and reports are useful administrative evidence, but they have limits.

14
00:01:37.780 --> 00:01:42.900
A log entry can show that an account performed an action from a context at a time.

15
00:01:43.000 --> 00:01:49.880
It does not automatically prove who was physically present, whether learning occurred, or whether competence was achieved.

16
00:01:49.980 --> 00:01:59.260
Completion and activity reports can support investigation, but they must be interpreted alongside course design, settings, identity controls, and known platform behavior.

17
00:01:59.540 --> 00:02:03.620
Security and privacy governance also depends on retention choices.

18
00:02:03.720 --> 00:02:13.080
Learning records, grades, submissions, messages, logs, backups, and exported data may have different retention needs.

19
00:02:13.180 --> 00:02:17.100
Keeping everything forever increases exposure and storage burden.

20
00:02:17.200 --> 00:02:22.320
Deleting too aggressively can harm academic, regulatory, or operational obligations.

21
00:02:22.420 --> 00:02:31.380
Administrators should not make these decisions alone; they should implement decisions owned by the right privacy, legal, academic, and business stakeholders.

22
00:02:31.660 --> 00:02:34.540
A mature evidence practice helps during incidents.

23
00:02:34.640 --> 00:02:51.040
When administrators can show the current version, recent changes, role assignments, plugin inventory, task status, backup history, restore-test results, relevant logs, and communication record, the organization can respond faster and with less guessing.

24
00:02:51.140 --> 00:02:54.900
Evidence should support decisions without pretending to prove more than it can.

25
00:02:55.000 --> 00:02:58.600
That restraint is part of trustworthy administration.
